  "evidence_classification": "supports",
  "interpretation": "In this controlled refined-interior portrait context, removing the `Hasselblad camera` phrase did not reduce visible photographic rendering quality when an 85mm portrait perspective and the rest of the scene structure were held constant. The no-brand condition scored 0.25 higher on the primary photographic_rendering_quality dimension, 0.25 higher on technical-defect cleanliness, and 0.125 higher overall, while semantic compliance and aesthetic quality were unchanged. Under the preregistered practical non-degradation threshold of -0.25 on the primary dimension, this run supports the hypothesis that the camera-brand phrase contributed little in this context. The result does not establish that camera-brand wording is universally useless across photographic tasks.",
